    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где мой сушист?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is my sushi chef?' You'll hear 'where' all the time in the show, here connected with 'sushi chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Откуда я знаю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How should I know?' Notice the word order: 'where from' comes first, and 'know' finishes the sentence. And Chef Viktor jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Утром он был здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He was here this morning.' Here 'this morning' sets up the idea, while 'here' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Может, он заболел.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe he got sick.' You'll hear 'can' all the time in the show, here connected with 'to fall ill'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Суши отменяются?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is sushi canceled?' Kristina starts with 'sushi' and pairs it with 'to be canceled' to make the point clear. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего не отменяется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nothing is canceled.' Chef Viktor puts 'nothing' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'is canceled'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "У нас восемь заказов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have eight orders.' Listen for the word meaning 'us' right alongside 'eight' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам всё сделаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will do everything myself.' Here 'I' sets up the idea, while 'I'll do' comes at the end for emphasis. Then Kristina replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Клиенты ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Customers are waiting.' You'll hear 'client' all the time in the show, here connected with 'are waiting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Идите отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out of here.' Notice the word order: 'come' comes first, and 'from here' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мы уже уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are already leaving.' Listen for the word meaning 'we' right alongside 'already' in this line. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Думай быстрее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Think fast.' The first content word means 'think', and 'faster' rounds out the thought. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
